.TH UNZIP 1 2026-10-19 tugz
.SH NAME
unzip, zipinfo \- list, test, and extract zip archives
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B unzip
.RI [ \-options ]
.IR file [ .zip ]
.RI [ member .\|.\|.]
.RB [ \-x
.IR member .\|.\|.]
.RB [ \-d
.IR dir ]
.br
.B zipinfo
.RB [ \-12smlvhtTzC ]
.IR file [ .zip ]
.RI [ member .\|.\|.]
.RB [ \-x
.IR member .\|.\|.]
.br
.B unzip \-Z
.RB [ \-12smlvhtTzC ]
.IR file [ .zip ]
.RI [ member .\|.\|.]
.RB [ \-x
.IR member .\|.\|.]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B unzip
extracts the entries of
.IR file ,
or those matching the
.IR member s,
into the current directory.
It is a subset of Info-ZIP UnZip 6.0, with the same options, messages,
listings, and exit statuses, plus busybox unzip's features.
Run as
.BR zipinfo ,
it lists archives instead (see
.BR "ZIPINFO MODE" ).
.PP
The archive is tried as named, then with
.B .zip
and
.B .ZIP
appended.
A name with wildcards processes each match.
An archive of
.B \-
is read from standard input, and then no file is replaced without
.BR \-o .
Members are patterns:
.B *
matches any run of characters,
.B /
included,
.B ?\&
one,
.B [.\|.\|.]
a set, and
.B \e
escapes (on Windows it separates, as
.B /
does).
.PP
Before replacing a file,
.B unzip
asks:
.BR y es,
.BR n o,
.BR A ll,
.BR N one,
or
.BR r ename.
.B A
and
.B N
answer for the rest of the run; the end of input answers
.BR N .
.PP
Extraction never writes outside the destination.
Leading
.B /
and
.B ..\&
components are dropped from names, and nothing is written through a
symbolic link, whether from the archive or already on disk.
A file whose data is damaged is removed rather than kept.
Entries may be stored, deflated, or Deflate64; encrypted entries, and
other methods, are skipped.
.SH OPTIONS
Letters combine
.RB ( \-qo ),
and a
.B \-
before one negates it
.RB ( \-\-q
cancels a
.B \-q
from
.BR UNZIP ).
As in busybox, an argument after the archive made only of option letters
is taken for options
.RB ( "unzip a.zip \-o" ).
.TP
.B \-l
List entries.
.TP
.B \-v
List entries verbosely; alone, show the version.
.TP
.B \-t
Test entries' data.
.TP
.B \-p
Extract to standard output, with no messages.
.TP
.B \-c
Extract to standard output, naming each entry.
.TP
.B \-z
Show only the archive comment.
.TP
.BI \-d " dir"
Extract into
.IR dir ,
which may come anywhere, or as
.BI \-d dir\fR.
.TP
.BI \-x " member\fR .\|.\|."
Exclude the members that follow.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-o
Never, or always, replace existing files.
.TP
.B \-f
Replace existing files with newer entries, creating none.
.TP
.B \-u
Like
.BR \-f ,
and also create new files.
.TP
.B \-j
Extract every entry into one directory, without its path.
.TP
.B \-C
Match members ignoring case.
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-DD
Restore no directory times, or no times at all.
.TP
.B \-V
Keep a
.BI ; N
version suffix.
.TP
.B \-K
Keep set-ID and sticky bits (POSIX).
.TP
.B \-X
Restore owners (POSIX).
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-qq
Quiet, quieter.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-hh
Usage, more help.
.TP
.B \-Z
ZipInfo mode; must come first.
.PP
Other UnZip options are refused (10) rather than ignored, among them
text conversion, passwords, and
.BR \-: .
.SH ZIPINFO MODE
Run by a name starting with
.B zipinfo
or
.B ii
(in any case), or with
.B \-Z
first,
.B unzip
lists entries as Info-ZIP's ZipInfo 3.00 does, in its formats.
Options come before the archive, and a
.B \-
negates the letter after it, even in the next argument.
The header and totals show by default, unless members are named.
.TP
.B \-1
Names only, one per line, with no header, totals, or comment.
.TP
.B \-2
Names, with the header, totals, or comment if asked for.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-m ", " \-l
As
.BR "ls \-l" :
short (the default), with the compression, or with the compressed size.
.TP
.B \-v
Every field of the end records and of each entry, extra fields decoded.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-t
The header lines; the totals line.
.TP
.B \-z
The archive comment.
.TP
.B \-T
Times as
.IR yyyymmdd.hhmmss .
.TP
.B \-C
Match members ignoring case.
.TP
.BI \-x " member\fR .\|.\|."
Exclude the members that follow, after the archive.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR UNZIP ", " UNZIPOPT
Options applied first, from
.BR UNZIP ,
or if it has none
.BR UNZIPOPT .
.TP
.BR ZIPINFO ", " ZIPINFOOPT
Likewise, in ZipInfo mode, where
.B UNZIP
is not read.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, in ZipInfo mode even with some members unmatched.
.TP
.B 1
Warnings.
.TP
.B 2
Errors in some entries.
.TP
.B 3
A damaged archive.
.TP
.B 4
Out of memory.
.TP
.B 9
No archive found.
.TP
.B 10
Bad or unsupported options.
.TP
.B 11
No matching entries.
.TP
.B 12
Overlapped entries (a zip bomb).
.TP
.B 50
A write error.
.TP
.B 51
An archive cut short.
.TP
.B 81
Entries skipped (encrypted, or an unsupported method).
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
unzip \-q release\-1.2.3.zip \-d dist
unzip \-p data.zip config.ini | grep port
unzip a.zip \-x \(aq*.o\(aq
zipinfo \-1 release\-1.2.3.zip
.fi
.SH NOTES
On Windows, names are decoded from the OEM or ANSI code page when not
UTF-8, characters Windows forbids become
.BR _ ,
and links are extracted as files holding their targets.
.B \-K
and
.B \-X
are refused there.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR zip (1),
.BR gzip (1)
